STEP 1: Nmap Port Scan
----------------------
Goal: Identify open ports and services on the target.

Command:
nmap -p 2082,2083,2086,2087,2095,2096 medirozahospital.com

Tool Used: Nmap

Result:
PORT     STATE    SERVICE
2082/tcp open     infowave
2083/tcp open     radsec
2086/tcp filtered gnunet
2087/tcp filtered eli
2095/tcp open     nbx-ser
2096/tcp open     nbx-dir

Findings:
- cPanel accessible on ports 2082 & 2083
- WHM filtered (firewall in place)
- Webmail accessible on ports 2095 & 2096


STEP 2: Directory Fuzzing
-------------------------
Goal: Discover hidden directories and files.

Command:
ffuf -u https://medirozahospital.com/FUZZ \
     -w /usr/share/wordlists/dirb/common.txt \
     -t 5 -p 1.0 -fc 404,403,429 -c

Tool Used: FFUF

Result:
/old/                   [Status: 301]
/staff/                 [Status: 301]
/patient/               [Status: 301]
/robots.txt             [Status: 200]
/wp-admin               [Status: 200]

Findings:
- /old/ directory likely contains backup files
- /patient/ is the patient portal
- /staff/ is the staff portal
- WordPress admin exists at /wp-admin


STEP 3: Robots.txt Analysis
---------------------------
Goal: Identify restricted/disallowed paths.

Command:
curl -sk https://medirozahospital.com/robots.txt

Tool Used: curl

Result:
User-agent: *
Disallow: /patient/
Disallow: /staff/
Disallow: /old/

Sitemap: https://medirozahospital.com/sitemap.xml

Findings:
- /old/ directory confirmed as backup location
- /patient/ and /staff/ portals identified
- Sitemap may reveal additional endpoints


